¶
{{smaller block|They cannot longer dwell upon the estate, but that remains ''unrifled'', and descends upon their heir.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Works (ed. 1835), I. 717.}}}}

'''unrig''' (un-rig′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''rig''<sup>2</sup>.] ''Naut.'', to strip, as a ship, of both standing and running rigging, etc. ''Dryden'', tr. of Juvenal’s Satires, xiv., note 24.

'''unrigged''' (un-rigd′), ''a.'' Without rigging; not rigged.
{{smaller block|Still ''unrigg’d'' his shatter’d vessels lie.
{{right|''Pitt'', Æneid, iv. (''Encyc. Dict.'')}}}}

'''unright''' (un-rīt′), ''a.'' [ME. ''unright'', ''unriht'', ''unriȝt'', ''unrigt'', < AS. ''unriht'' (= OS. ''unreht'' = OFries. ''unriucht'', ''onriucht'' = MLG. ''unrecht'' = D. ''onregt'' = OHG. MHG. ''unreht'', G. ''unrecht'' = Icel. ''ūrēttr'' = Norw. ''urett'' = Sw. ''orätt'' = Dan. ''uret''), wrong, not right, < ''un-'', not, + ''riht'', right: see ''un-''<sup>1</sup> and ''right'', ''a.''] Not right; unrighteous; unjust; wrong.
{{smaller block|<poem>Late hem neuer ther to haue myȝt,
For sikirli hit were ''vnryȝt''.</poem>
{{right|''King Horn'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 86.}}
A rightful Prince by ''unright'' deeds a Tyrant groweth.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arber’s Eng. Garner, I. 566.}}}}

'''unright''' (un-rīt′), ''n.'' [ME. ''unright'', < AS. ''unriht'' (= OS. ''unreht'' = OFries. ''unriucht'', ''onriucht'' = MLG. ''unrecht'' = OHG. MHG. ''unreht'', G. ''unrecht'' = Norw. ''urett'', ''orett'' = Sw. ''orätt'' = Dan. ''uret''), wrong, injustice, sin, < ''un-'', not, + ''riht'', right, justice: see ''un-''<sup>1</sup> and ''right'', ''n.''] That which is unright or not right; wrong; injustice. [Obsolete or archaic.]
{{smaller block|Certes, I dide yow nevere ''unright''.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Wife of Bath’s Tale, l. 237.}}
That particular form of unlaw and ''unright'' which con-}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|sisted in abusing the King’s authority to wring money out of all classes. {{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Norm. Conq., V. 108.}}}}

'''unright†''' (un-rīt′), ''adv.'' [ME. ''unright'', < AS. ''unrihte'' (= D. ''onregt'' = OS. OHG. ''unrehto'', MHG. ''unrehte''), wrongly, crookedly, unjustly, < ''un-'', not, + ''rihte'', straight, right: see ''un-''<sup>1</sup> and ''right'', ''adv.''] Wrongly.
{{smaller block|The sonne wente his course ''unright''.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, v. 661.}}}}

'''unright†''' (un-rīt′), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''unrighten''; < ''unright'', ''a.''] To make wrong. ''Gower'', Conf. Amant., ii.

'''unrighteous''' (un-rī′tyus), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unrihtwis'', ''unrigtwis'', < AS. ''unrihtwīs'' (= Icel. ''ūrēttvīss''), not righteous, < ''un-'', not, + ''rihtwīs'', righteous: see ''un-''<sup>1</sup> and ''righteous''.] Not righteous; unjust; not equitable; evil; wicked; not honest or upright: of persons or things.
{{smaller block|Deliver me out of the hand of the ''unrighteous''.
{{right|Ps. lxxi. 4.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn.''' ''Ungodly'', ''Impious'', etc. (see ''irreligious''); wrong, unjust, unfair, iniquitous, sinful.}}

'''unrighteously''' (un-rī′tyus-li), ''adv.'' [< ME. *''unrightwisely''; < ''unrighteous'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In an unrighteous manner; unjustly; wickedly; sinfully.
{{smaller block|<poem>You gods, I see that who ''unrighteously''
Holds wealth or state from others shall be curs’d
In that which meaner men are blest withal.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Philaster, ii. 4.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''unrighteousness''' (un-rī′t̮yus-nes), ''n.'' The character or state of being unrighteous; injustice; a violation of the divine law, or of the principles of justice and equity; wickedness.

'''unrightful''' (un-rīt′fu̇l), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unrihtful'', ''onrigtvolle''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''rightful''.] '''1.''' Not rightful; unjust; not consonant with justice.
{{smaller block|Victorie of ''unryghtful'' deth.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Boëthius, i. prose 3.}}}}
'''2.''' Not having right; not legitimate.
{{smaller block|And he shall think that thou, which know’st the way
To plant ''unrightful'' kings, wilt know again.
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II., v. i. 63.}}}}

'''unrightfully''' (un-rīt′fu̇l-i), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''unryghtfully''; < ''unrightful'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] Unjustly; unrighteously.
{{smaller block|Anoyinge foolk treden, and that ''unryghtfully'', on the nekkes of hooly men. ''Chaucer'', Boëthius, i. meter 5.}}

'''unrightfulness''' (un-rīt′fu̇l-nes), ''n.'' [< ME. ''unrihtfulnesse''; < ''unrightful'' + ''-ness''.] The character or state of being unrightful. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|We must beware of seeking to extenuate his [the unjust Judge’s] ''unrightfulness.''
{{right|''Trench'', On the Parables, p. 372.}}}}

'''unring''' (un-ring′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''ring''<sup>1</sup>.] To deprive of a ring; remove a ring from.

'''unringed''' (un-ringd′), ''a.'' Not having a ring, as in the nose.
{{smaller block|Pigs ''unringed.'' ''S. Butler'', Hudibras, ii. 2.}}

'''unrioted†''' (un-rī′ọt-ed), ''a.'' Free from rioting; not disgraced by riot. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|A chaste, ''unrioted'' house.
{{right|''May'', tr. of Lucan’s Pharsalia, ix.}}}}
